WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:01.511
- Good Morning Believe Nation!

00:00:01.511 --> 00:00:04.014
Today's message is Hustle.

00:00:04.014 --> 00:00:05.975
Over to you, Jennifer Lawrence.

00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:08.201
(rooster crowing)

00:00:08.201 --> 00:00:10.816
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:10.816 --> 00:00:13.155
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:13.155 --> 00:00:17.487
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:17.487 --> 00:00:19.488
- The interesting thing,
for me, to talk to you,

00:00:19.488 --> 00:00:22.712
and what makes you so
fascinating is the talent

00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:24.784
that you see on the screen.

00:00:24.784 --> 00:00:28.408
But, at the same time, there
is a certain innocence,

00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:32.579
and yet a certain power,
and it seems to me that,

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:34.912
it's both artist and also...

00:00:37.376 --> 00:00:38.631
business.

00:00:38.631 --> 00:00:39.994
- Oh, thank you.

00:00:39.994 --> 00:00:41.042
- [Charlie] But, do you believe that?

00:00:41.042 --> 00:00:42.277
- Yes.
- Am I right?

00:00:42.277 --> 00:00:44.200
- I've worked very hard to make that.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.257
- [Charlie] To make it just that.

00:00:45.257 --> 00:00:47.519
- To make that true, so, thank you.

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:48.667
- [Charlie] That it's both that.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:49.500
- Thank you.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.007
Well because without
gaining some sort of control

00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:56.445
over the business, I lose some
control over the creative.

00:00:56.445 --> 00:00:58.120
Which is most important, so, I--

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.302
- [Charlie] And the more you
control over the business,

00:00:59.302 --> 00:01:01.115
the more you can give
flower to the creative.

00:01:01.115 --> 00:01:02.426
- Exactly.

00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:03.629
- [Charlie] Or give wings to the creative.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:06.123
- Yeah, so I used to stay out of it,

00:01:06.123 --> 00:01:08.628
I don't care, I don't
want to, I'm an artist.

00:01:08.628 --> 00:01:10.563
I don't need it, you know, and,

00:01:10.563 --> 00:01:12.644
but this is my business now, and it's my,

00:01:12.644 --> 00:01:15.164
it is important if my agent,
my agent always says I'm

00:01:15.164 --> 00:01:19.256
his only client that ever
calls him back as soon as he,

00:01:19.256 --> 00:01:21.317
because it's my business
and I respect my business.

00:01:21.317 --> 00:01:22.536
- [Charlie] Your agent
says you're the only client

00:01:22.536 --> 00:01:24.678
that calls him back to say.
- Immediately.

00:01:24.678 --> 00:01:25.511
- [Charlie] Immediately.

00:01:25.511 --> 00:01:26.379
- If I have a missed call, I call him,

00:01:26.379 --> 00:01:28.471
I'm probably the only, I return emails,

00:01:28.471 --> 00:01:30.043
I return phone calls, because--

00:01:30.043 --> 00:01:32.594
- [Charlie] Because you want
to know what options there are?

00:01:32.594 --> 00:01:34.971
- Yes, I've worked
really hard to build this

00:01:34.971 --> 00:01:36.741
and I want to continue building it,

00:01:36.741 --> 00:01:39.288
and it's my business,
my personal business.

00:01:39.288 --> 00:01:42.030
So, I don't understand
how people do slack.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.411
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:01:43.411 --> 00:01:45.999
The question of the day
today is, I'm curious:

00:01:45.999 --> 00:01:50.669
How much are you guys hustling
in your business right now?

00:01:50.669 --> 00:01:52.215
How much are you hustling?

00:01:52.215 --> 00:01:53.309
Leave it in the comments below,

00:01:53.309 --> 00:01:54.900
I'm going to join in on the discussion.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.610
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Yong Yu Liu.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.579
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:01.895
Your One Word, it really,
really, really, really

00:02:01.895 --> 00:02:02.851
means a lot to me.

00:02:02.851 --> 00:02:04.221
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:02:04.221 --> 00:02:05.425
I believe in you.

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:07.635
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:02:07.635 --> 00:02:09.445
and whatever your one word is.

00:02:09.445 --> 00:02:11.983
Much love, and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:02:11.983 --> 00:02:14.142
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:02:14.142 --> 00:02:16.995
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:02:16.995 --> 00:02:20.695
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:02:20.695 --> 00:02:22.578
- Hey Believe Nation, if
you want a little more

00:02:22.578 --> 00:02:26.457
hustle motivation stay tuned
for some special bonus clips.

00:02:26.457 --> 00:02:29.081
- I worked for Ms. Hughes
for about eight months

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:32.351
and I got a weekend gig in New York.

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:35.990
So, I would do weekdays
at WOL in Washington

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.951
and drive up on Fridays after my shift,

00:02:37.951 --> 00:02:39.368
which would end at 7 o'clock

00:02:39.368 --> 00:02:42.857
and I'd be on all weekend
long over in Queens,

00:02:42.857 --> 00:02:45.450
that's where the radio
station was located.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.086
And then I would drive back to D.C.

00:02:48.086 --> 00:02:50.292
And I would never even
stop at my parents house,

00:02:50.292 --> 00:02:51.682
never had time for friends,

00:02:51.682 --> 00:02:55.025
because everybody was kind of,
not understanding my dream,

00:02:55.025 --> 00:02:57.129
and kind of laughing at me
or something, or trying to,

00:02:57.129 --> 00:03:00.232
you know my friends were like
real immature and stupid,

00:03:00.232 --> 00:03:01.760
and wanting to party and hang out,

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.128
and I'm on a path.

00:03:03.128 --> 00:03:05.120
And my parents were very, you know,

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.878
"Ohhh, do you want something to eat?"

00:03:07.878 --> 00:03:09.597
"What is this crazy thing
you're doing called radio?"

00:03:09.597 --> 00:03:10.832
I would sleep in my Subaru,

00:03:10.832 --> 00:03:14.132
they gave me a Subaru
for college graduation,

00:03:14.132 --> 00:03:18.282
and I would sleep in my car
at every rest stop between

00:03:18.282 --> 00:03:20.828
New York and Washington D.C.

00:03:20.828 --> 00:03:23.904
to block out the noise
of people questioning--

00:03:23.904 --> 00:03:24.740
- Telling you you can't do it.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:25.668
- Right!

00:03:25.668 --> 00:03:27.404
I would literally be,

00:03:27.404 --> 00:03:31.404
I'd have a blanket, an
alarm clock, and a quilt.

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:35.311
And I would take a bird
bath in the bathroom.

00:03:35.311 --> 00:03:38.936
I'd be woken up by the man
sweeping next to my Subaru,

00:03:38.936 --> 00:03:41.027
looking at me, 'cause I'm
like the crazy, you know,

00:03:41.027 --> 00:03:43.666
girl in the, and I had a
baby shade without a baby,

00:03:43.666 --> 00:03:45.967
pulled the baby shade down, you know.

00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:48.894
And that was my life for about two months

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:50.227
I was going back and forth.

00:03:50.227 --> 00:03:52.073
- How'd you get an agent?

00:03:52.073 --> 00:03:54.823
- Oh, I got to, let me tell you...

00:03:56.543 --> 00:03:58.732
Talk about some name-dropping, right?

00:03:58.732 --> 00:04:01.698
So, I had a friend that at the time

00:04:01.698 --> 00:04:04.798
was good friends with
Keenen Ivory Wayans, okay.

00:04:04.798 --> 00:04:08.849
And, I got to meet Keenen and
we became very good friends,

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:10.971
I ended up doing A Low Down
Dirty Shame with Keenen.

00:04:10.971 --> 00:04:11.804
- Oh, sure yeah.

00:04:11.804 --> 00:04:13.625
- But, I didn't have an agent.
- As Peaches.

00:04:13.625 --> 00:04:14.972
As Peaches, right.

00:04:14.972 --> 00:04:17.364
I didn't have an agent yet.

00:04:17.364 --> 00:04:20.622
And I was like, you know, how am I?

00:04:20.622 --> 00:04:24.811
I was just going through
the list of agencies,

00:04:24.811 --> 00:04:26.400
and I was like, I got to get an agent.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.665
And so, I literally called this one agent,

00:04:28.665 --> 00:04:30.956
her name was Nancy Rainford.

00:04:30.956 --> 00:04:35.156
And I said, "I'm a really good
friend of Keenen Ivory Wayans

00:04:35.156 --> 00:04:38.820
"and he told me to call you specifically."

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:43.153
And she's like this
small agency, on Melrose,

00:04:43.153 --> 00:04:45.005
but you know, she had me,

00:04:45.005 --> 00:04:46.773
she had Michael Rapaport at the time.

00:04:46.773 --> 00:04:47.606
- Ah wow.

00:04:47.606 --> 00:04:49.689
- Yeah, she had Michael
Rapaport at the time, so I said,

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:52.275
"He told me to call you specifically."

00:04:52.275 --> 00:04:54.038
You know so I'm runnin' this whole game,

00:04:54.038 --> 00:04:55.287
and she was like, really?

00:04:55.287 --> 00:04:57.765
She was like, well, why
don't you come in and see me?

00:04:57.765 --> 00:04:59.518
So, I went in to see her,

00:04:59.518 --> 00:05:02.685
and I put on the jazz hands, you know,

00:05:04.287 --> 00:05:05.206
and she was like,

00:05:05.206 --> 00:05:08.256
you know what, I like you,
I'm going to represent you.

00:05:08.256 --> 00:05:11.580
And I'm going to tell you
she was an awesome agent,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:12.965
she was an awesome agent,

00:05:12.965 --> 00:05:15.021
and she had Michael Rapaport and I,

00:05:15.021 --> 00:05:17.636
and she started my career.

00:05:17.636 --> 00:05:18.469
- Wow.
She started my career.

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:20.500
And then she eventually
got out of the business.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.710
But, she was the one that
started my whole career.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:24.986
- And it was just that--
And I got in the door

00:05:24.986 --> 00:05:27.508
because of Keenen's name.

00:05:27.508 --> 00:05:29.030
- That attitude, too.
- That attitude.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.958
I was like he told me you specifically.

00:05:32.958 --> 00:05:34.092
- That's good, that's
actually a very good.

00:05:34.092 --> 00:05:34.975
- Isn't that good, yeah.

00:05:34.975 --> 00:05:37.392
- That is actually something.

00:05:38.799 --> 00:05:40.177
That's great, that's great,

00:05:40.177 --> 00:05:41.744
I love that.
- Yeah, I did, I did.

00:05:41.744 --> 00:05:43.952
Look, if it's not one
thing Baltimore taught me,

00:05:43.952 --> 00:05:48.253
it definitely taught me how
to hustle, I'll tell you that.

00:05:48.253 --> 00:05:49.783
- You know people talk about
how you need a vacation

00:05:49.783 --> 00:05:52.441
or something, I don't,
a vacation from what?

00:05:52.441 --> 00:05:53.791
I love my life.

00:05:53.791 --> 00:05:55.692
I feel, I'm healthy, thank God.

00:05:55.692 --> 00:05:58.519
And, you know I have
these incredible directors

00:05:58.519 --> 00:06:00.890
that are interested in working with me,

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.042
and I'm going to jump at that,

00:06:03.042 --> 00:06:04.463
as long as there's enough time to prep

00:06:04.463 --> 00:06:06.070
and I feel like I can add to it,

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.284
you know, yeah, yeah for sure.

00:06:08.284 --> 00:06:09.117
- [Interviewer] Has it changed in terms,

00:06:09.117 --> 00:06:10.300
I guess there's a point

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.788
where you don't have
to chase quite as hard?

00:06:13.788 --> 00:06:15.163
- You know it depends on the project.

00:06:15.163 --> 00:06:17.606
I still would say, I wrote
an email to a director

00:06:17.606 --> 00:06:19.283
about six months ago about a project

00:06:19.283 --> 00:06:23.075
that I just felt so connected
to and never got a response.

00:06:23.075 --> 00:06:24.353
- [Interviewer] That's surprising.

00:06:24.353 --> 00:06:27.146
- Yeah, but you know, you
never, you know, it's yeah.

00:06:27.146 --> 00:06:28.350
- [Interviewer] You'd just
think that professional courtesy

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:29.183
would be enough to--

00:06:29.183 --> 00:06:32.056
- Oh, not in this business.

00:06:32.056 --> 00:06:35.796
- From start to finish it
takes about nine months to,

00:06:35.796 --> 00:06:38.235
sometimes longer, sometimes
you know, 10 months.

00:06:38.235 --> 00:06:40.661
It's roughly a year between the time

00:06:40.661 --> 00:06:42.584
that the story is pitched

00:06:42.584 --> 00:06:44.238
to the time that it gets on the air.

00:06:44.238 --> 00:06:45.464
It takes roughly a year to do it.

00:06:45.464 --> 00:06:48.139
And it's, you know, no
one has ever figured out

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:51.995
a quicker way to do hand-drawn
animation in prime time,

00:06:51.995 --> 00:06:53.337
and it's all still hand-drawn.

00:06:53.337 --> 00:06:54.723
It's colored with computers,

00:06:54.723 --> 00:06:57.503
but every single frame
is still done by hand.

00:06:57.503 --> 00:07:00.000
You know this season is a little
smoother than last season.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.729
And last season was little
smoother than the one before,

00:07:01.729 --> 00:07:03.951
so it's, it gets incrementally easier.

00:07:03.951 --> 00:07:06.554
It's still in many ways
a seven day a week job

00:07:06.554 --> 00:07:07.842
to get this thing done, I mean it's,

00:07:07.842 --> 00:07:11.388
it's a virtually impossible thing to do

00:07:11.388 --> 00:07:13.959
a prime time animated series, yet somehow,

00:07:13.959 --> 00:07:15.924
there's a bunch of people that do it.

00:07:15.924 --> 00:07:16.757
I, I...

00:07:17.925 --> 00:07:19.215
You know,

00:07:19.215 --> 00:07:20.545
it's, it's...

00:07:20.545 --> 00:07:22.853
I don't know how The Simpson's does it

00:07:22.853 --> 00:07:26.180
for as long a they've
been doing it, but it's a,

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:29.664
it's just a very, very
intense process that just

00:07:29.664 --> 00:07:32.855
in many ways, gets easier
when you get canceled.

